<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="hkg-conventions">
  <title>Scope and conventions</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>These notes develop the geometry of smooth compact irreducible holomorphic symplectic manifolds, including their relation to hyperkähler metrics, their deformations and periods, and their realization as moduli spaces. The examples and moduli constructions are part of the theory: they explain which lattice classes, birational models, and fibrations actually occur.</p>
  </introduction>
  <section xml:id="hkg-reading">
    <title>Background and references</title>
    <p>We assume differential forms and connections, compact Kähler Hodge theory, basic deformation theory, and the language of smooth projective varieties and coherent sheaves.</p>
    <p>The foundational references are Beauville <xref ref="hkg-bib-beauville"/> and Huybrechts <xref ref="hkg-bib-huybrechts"/>. For global Torelli and monodromy we use Markman <xref ref="hkg-bib-markman-survey"/> and Huybrechts <xref ref="hkg-bib-huybrechts-torelli"/>; for sheaf moduli we use Mukai, Yoshioka, and Huybrechts–Lehn. A citation to Huybrechts's <em>Basic results</em> with a subsection number refers to the linked 1997 preprint, unless stated otherwise. Its projectivity argument must be read with the erratum <xref ref="hkg-bib-huybrechts-erratum"/>.<fn>The erratum corrects an auxiliary positivity assertion and the proof of projectivity. It does not retract the projectivity criterion.</fn></p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="hkg-notation">
    <title>Notation</title>
    <p>Dimension means complex dimension. An IHS manifold is denoted by <m>X</m>, with <m>\dim X=2n</m>; its underlying smooth manifold may be denoted by <m>M</m>. The broader metric meaning of <em>hyperkähler</em> is defined in <xref ref="hkg-metric"/>. A hyperkähler metric has real dimension <m>4n</m>. Projectivity is imposed only when stated.</p>
    <p>We write <m>H^k(X,A)</m> for singular cohomology with coefficients in <m>A</m>; an omitted coefficient ring means <m>\Q</m>. The superscript is the ordinary cohomological degree. The Hodge component <m>H^{p,q}(X)</m> is a subspace of <m>H^{p+q}(X,\C)</m>. Products of cohomology classes are cup products; integrals mean evaluation on the complex orientation. Chern classes have <m>c_i(E)\in H^{2i}(X,\Z)</m>.</p>
    <p>The primitive integral Beauville–Bogomolov–Fujiki bilinear form is denoted by <m>q_X(-,-)</m>, and <m>q_X(a)=q_X(a,a)</m>. We use the same symbol after extension of scalars. Its normalization and the Fujiki constant <m>c_X</m> are fixed in <xref ref="hkg-bbf"/>. A rescaled form is written with a different symbol and its scaling factor is specified.</p>
    <p>A holomorphic symplectic form is denoted by <m>\sigma</m>. It is chosen only up to a nonzero scalar unless a normalization is stated. A Kähler form and its cohomology class are both denoted by <m>\omega</m> when no confusion is possible. For a hyperkähler metric, <m>I,J,K</m> are parallel complex structures and <m>\omega_I,\omega_J,\omega_K</m> are their Kähler forms.</p>
    <p>For a compact Kähler manifold, <m>\Pic(X)</m> is the group of holomorphic line bundles and <m>\NS(X)=\Pic(X)/\Pic^0(X)</m>. Its rank <m>\rho(X):=\operatorname{rk}\NS(X)</m> is the <em>Picard number</em>. For IHS manifolds <m>\Pic^0(X)=0</m>, and the first Chern class identifies <m>\Pic(X)</m> with <m>H^2(X,\Z)\cap H^{1,1}(X)</m>; this is proved in <xref ref="hkg-bbf-picard"/>. A lattice is a free abelian group of finite rank with an integral nondegenerate symmetric bilinear form. When a Picard group is called a lattice, nondegeneracy of its restricted form must be checked: it can fail on a nonprojective manifold.</p>
  </section>
</chapter>
